From: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: AQM eviction: sync generic flow_offload teardown

AQM v2 phase A (summarized in docs/README.md). qos-06's eviction
(mtk_qdma_aqm_unbind_queue()) calls mtk_foe_entry_clear() directly on
the PPE hardware FOE entry, entirely bypassing the generic kernel
flowtable (net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c) the flow's
struct flow_offload object lives in. That object's own bookkeeping -
NF_FLOW_HW, IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT on the conntrack entry, flow->timeout -
is left completely untouched: nf_flowtable keeps believing the flow is
still actively, currently hardware-offloaded.

Traced the real consequence in the generic flowtable source, not
inferred: nf_flow_table.h defines NF_FLOW_TIMEOUT as 30*HZ, and
nf_flow_table_core.c's gc_work runs every HZ but only tears a flow down
once nf_flow_has_expired() is true - 30s of apparent inactivity. Nothing
about qos-06's own grace_ms or the live rate/drop trigger state feeds
into that decision. Once that 30s generic timer (not the AQM) finally
tears the zombie flow down and a fresh packet re-triggers
flow_offload_add(), the flow is immediately hardware-offload-eligible
again with no relationship to whether the WAN queue is still congested.

Fix: call the same flow_offload_teardown() every other teardown path in
the kernel already uses (net/sched/act_ct.c is the only other non-core
caller; no in-tree hardware-offload driver does this today - surveyed
bnxt_tc.c, mlx5 en_tc.c/tc_ct.c, nfp flower offload.c/conntrack.c,
sfc tc*.c, mlxsw, ocelot_net.c, and others, none call back into
flow_offload_teardown()/flow_offload_lookup() to request their own
flow's removal). This immediately clears IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT and marks
NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN, so nf_flowtable's own belief about this flow matches
reality the instant qos-06 evicts it, instead of drifting for up to
30s. The struct flow_offload is resolved from entry->cookie, which is
exactly the tuple pointer nf_flow_table_offload.c hands the driver as
flow_cls_offload->cookie ("cls_flow->cookie = (unsigned long)tuple") -
the identical container_of() flow_offload_lookup() itself performs from
a fresh rhashtable lookup, just recovered from the pointer already on
hand instead of a second lookup.

Deliberately NOT routed through the generic nf_flow_offload_del() async
workqueue path (net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c): that symbol
isn't exported for use outside net/netfilter/*.c, and even if it were,
its own gc_work-driven dispatch would add up to ~1s of extra
hardware-still-bound latency before the real FOE invalidate happens -
exactly the delay this AQM exists to avoid. The synchronous
mtk_foe_entry_clear() call stays exactly as it was; this patch only
adds the generic-bookkeeping sync alongside it.

Memory safety verified by tracing the actual free path, not assumed:
flow_offload_free() (nf_flow_table_core.c) frees via kfree_rcu(). RCU
grace periods are global, not per-rhashtable - holding rcu_read_lock()
anywhere (which rhashtable_walk_start() already does for this walk)
blocks any kfree_rcu()-scheduled reclaim everywhere, the identical
guarantee nf_flow_offload_gc_step() itself relies on to dereference
flow->ct directly from its own (different) rhashtable walk with no
extra reference. flow_offload_teardown() itself takes no blocking locks
(bitops and a WRITE_ONCE; verified by reading its body) and is
idempotent, so it's safe to call from this BH/RCU context even against
an already-torn-down flow.

Build-verified the hard way: an initial version of this patch called
flow_offload_teardown() directly and failed vmlinux link
("undefined reference to `flow_offload_teardown'") - this tree's config
has CONFIG_NET_MEDIATEK_SOC=y (vmlinux built-in) but
CONFIG_NF_FLOW_TABLE=m (loadable module), so a direct call from this
driver into that module's exported symbol can never link. Resolved with
symbol_get()/EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL dynamic lookup instead of a direct call -
see the in-code comment on mtk_qdma_aqm_flow_teardown() for the full
mechanism and its cached-pointer/module-reference lifetime. Recording
this here because it's exactly the kind of constraint no amount of
source reading catches without an actual compile.

Not in scope here: this driver's own eth->flow_table bookkeeping entry
(the mtk_flow_entry struct itself) still isn't freed by qos-06 - it's
already skipped as inert via the existing entry->hash == 0xffff guard
and only reclaimed later via mtk_flow_offload_destroy()'s own full
teardown. Phase B (a following patch) adds the ct-mark hold/release
state machine that makes the eviction actually stick instead of
relying on the same 30s timer for re-offload eligibility; this patch
only fixes the bookkeeping desync.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -36,6 +36,8 @@
 #include <net/checksum.h>
 #include <net/page_pool/helpers.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h>
 
 #include "mtk_eth_soc.h"
 #include "mtk_wed.h"
@@ -1444,6 +1446,66 @@
  * the queue/score, which would cost a second ppe_lock'd stats read per
  * candidate for a result pass 1 already computed.
  */
+/* Resolve the generic struct flow_offload this FOE entry belongs to and
+ * tear it down through the same generic API path every other flow_offload
+ * teardown in the kernel uses.
+ *
+ * entry->cookie is exactly the tuple pointer nf_flow_table_offload.c hands
+ * the driver as flow_cls_offload->cookie (net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c:
+ * "cls_flow->cookie = (unsigned long)tuple") - the same pointer
+ * flow_offload_lookup() itself resolves via an identical container_of() from
+ * a fresh rhashtable lookup (net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c). Recovering
+ * it here from the cookie we already stored avoids a second lookup.
+ *
+ * Safe to dereference without an extra reference: struct flow_offload is
+ * freed via kfree_rcu() (flow_offload_free(), nf_flow_table_core.c), and
+ * this is called from within the same RCU read-side critical section
+ * rhashtable_walk_start() already holds for eth->flow_table below - RCU
+ * grace periods are global, so no kfree_rcu()-scheduled free anywhere can
+ * complete while any reader (this one included) holds the read lock, the
+ * same guarantee nf_flow_offload_gc_step() itself relies on to dereference
+ * flow->ct directly from its own, different, rhashtable walk. Idempotent
+ * against an already-torn-down flow (flow_offload_teardown() just sets
+ * flags/clears a status bit; safe to call twice).
+ *
+ * flow_offload_teardown() lives in nf_flow_table.ko (CONFIG_NF_FLOW_TABLE=m
+ * in this tree's config), while this driver (CONFIG_NET_MEDIATEK_SOC=y) is
+ * linked into vmlinux - a direct call would fail at vmlinux link time
+ * (confirmed: "undefined reference to `flow_offload_teardown'" against a
+ * real build before this dynamic-lookup form was written). Resolve it via
+ * symbol_get() instead: an EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL lookup by name against
+ * currently-loaded modules, cached after the first successful resolution
+ * (which also takes a permanent module reference on nf_flow_table,
+ * preventing it from ever being unloaded out from under the cached pointer -
+ * acceptable here since nf_flow_table is already a hard functional
+ * prerequisite for PPE hardware flow offload to work at all on this board).
+ * Graceful no-op if nf_flow_table isn't loaded yet (e.g. transiently early
+ * in boot, before fw4/nftables has loaded it) - falls back to exactly qos-06
+ * through qos-17's pre-existing mtk_foe_entry_clear()-only behavior for
+ * that call, not a hard failure.
+ */
+static void mtk_qdma_aqm_flow_teardown(struct mtk_flow_entry *entry)
+{
+	static void (*teardown_fn)(struct flow_offload *flow);
+	struct flow_offload_tuple *tuple = (void *)entry->cookie;
+	struct flow_offload_tuple_rhash *tuplehash;
+	struct flow_offload *flow;
+
+	if (!tuple)
+		return;
+
+	if (!teardown_fn) {
+		teardown_fn = symbol_get(flow_offload_teardown);
+		if (!teardown_fn)
+			return;
+	}
+
+	tuplehash = container_of(tuple, struct flow_offload_tuple_rhash, tuple);
+	flow = container_of(tuplehash, struct flow_offload, tuplehash[tuple->dir]);
+
+	teardown_fn(flow);
+}
+
 static unsigned int
 mtk_qdma_aqm_unbind_queue(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned int queue,
 			  unsigned int max)
@@ -1474,6 +1536,14 @@
 		if (!mtk_qdma_aqm_hash_selected(top, n_top, entry->hash))
 			continue;
 
+		/* Sync the generic nf_flowtable's own bookkeeping (IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT,
+		 * NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN) with the hardware invalidate below, instead of
+		 * leaving it to drift until nf_flowtable's own 30s idle GC
+		 * (NF_FLOW_TIMEOUT) notices on its own - see qos-18's commit
+		 * message for the full reasoning.
+		 */
+		mtk_qdma_aqm_flow_teardown(entry);
+
 		/* mtk_foe_entry_clear() takes ppe_lock (BH spinlock).
 		 * Calling under the RCU read lock from rhashtable_walk is valid;
 		 * ppe_lock and RCU do not conflict.  The entry is RCU-protected
